-- =====================================================================
-- Asad's Library Portal: book descriptions
-- Run once in Supabase: SQL Editor -> New query -> paste -> Run.
-- =====================================================================

-- A short, spoiler-free description for every book, shown in its pop-up.
-- These are original texts written for this library (two or three
-- sentences each), not publishers' blurbs, which are copyrighted.

alter table public.books add column description text;

update public.books as b
set description = v.description
from (values
  (1, 'Writer Ben Mears returns to the small Maine town where he grew up, hoping to lay an old childhood fear to rest. But something has moved into the Marsten House on the hill, and one by one the townsfolk begin to change. Stephen King''s take on the vampire story.'),
  (2, 'Psychotherapist Amy Morin names the habits that quietly hold people back, from wasting energy on what you can''t control to dreading time alone, and explains how to drop them. Short, practical chapters built on everyday examples.'),
  (3, 'A classic paperback anthology of short fiction from the 19th and 20th centuries, with stories by Hemingway, Poe, Joyce, Steinbeck and many more. Made for dipping into one story at a time.'),
  (4, 'Jonathan Clements tells Japan''s story from its earliest myths and emperors, through samurai, shoguns and centuries of isolation, to rapid modernisation and the present day. Brisk, readable and full of anecdotes.'),
  (5, 'Damon Knight''s 1962 anthology traces a hundred years of science fiction through its big ideas: robots, time travel, aliens, other worlds and more. Each theme gathers stories from the genre''s early pioneers to its golden age.'),
  (6, 'Westeros is torn apart as rival claimants fight for the Iron Throne, while a red comet blazes across the sky and the danger beyond the Wall keeps growing. Book two of A Song of Ice and Fire.'),
  (7, 'The fifth Song of Ice and Fire book picks up the threads left out of A Feast for Crows, following Jon, Daenerys and Tyrion far from King''s Landing as new powers gather in the East and the North.'),
  (8, 'The realm counts the cost of years of war. Book four follows Cersei, Jaime, Brienne and Arya, and brings in new players from the Iron Islands and Dorne, as the Seven Kingdoms struggle to hold together.'),
  (9, 'Lord Eddard Stark is called south to serve the king, drawing his family into the deadly politics of the capital. Across the sea an exiled princess dreams of her birthright, and beyond the Wall an ancient threat stirs. The first book of A Song of Ice and Fire.'),
  (10, 'A compact survey of Japanese history by R.H.P. Mason and J.G. Caiger, from prehistoric times to the modern era. Written as a clear introduction for students and general readers.'),
  (11, 'Simon Jenkins races through 2,500 years of European history, from ancient Athens to the present day, in a single short volume. Sharp, opinionated and good for seeing the big picture.'),
  (12, 'The war for the throne grinds on, alliances shift and old debts come due, while the Night''s Watch faces the wildlings massing beyond the Wall. Book three of A Song of Ice and Fire, and for many readers the best.'),
  (13, 'Dickens''s novel of London and Paris during the French Revolution follows a family drawn into the Terror, and a dissolute lawyer who finds an unexpected purpose. It opens with one of the most famous first lines in English.'),
  (14, 'A book of short daily readings, one for every day of the year, meant to build a more hopeful and grateful outlook. Each entry offers a thought or prompt to carry through the day.'),
  (15, 'Will is thirty-six, idle and happily childless, until he invents a son to meet single mothers and ends up tangled in the life of Marcus, an awkward twelve-year-old with troubles of his own. Funny, warm Nick Hornby.'),
  (16, 'Fresh out of prison, Shadow Moon takes a job as bodyguard to the mysterious Mr Wednesday and is pulled into a war between the old gods that immigrants brought to America and the new gods of media and technology. Neil Gaiman''s road-trip epic.'),
  (17, 'Tolstoy''s great novel of love, marriage and society in 19th-century Russia, set between Anna''s passionate affair and the landowner Levin''s search for meaning on his estate. Often called the greatest novel ever written.'),
  (18, 'An intermediate German coursebook and workbook from Langenscheidt, for learners moving beyond the basics (around level B1+). Each unit combines texts, grammar and exercises.'),
  (19, 'The German edition of Edward St Aubyn''s A Clue to the Exit. A burned-out screenwriter, told he has months to live, heads for the casino in Cannes and sets out to write a novel about consciousness. Dark, witty and philosophical.'),
  (20, 'Paramahansa Yogananda''s account of his childhood and spiritual search in India, the teachers who shaped him, and his mission to bring yoga and meditation to the West. A classic of 20th-century spiritual writing.'),
  (21, 'Book three of the Witcher saga. Wounded and hunted, Geralt sets out to find Ciri with an unlikely band of companions, crossing a land being torn apart by war.'),
  (22, 'Anne Lamott''s funny, honest guide to writing and the writing life: short assignments, permission to write terrible first drafts, jealousy, doubt, and getting through it all one small piece at a time.'),
  (23, 'The first Witcher novel. Geralt takes young Ciri to the witchers'' fortress of Kaer Morhen to train, while kings, spies and mages begin to hunt her for the power in her blood.'),
  (24, 'In the World State, people are engineered in hatcheries, conditioned into contentment and kept happy with the drug soma. Aldous Huxley''s 1932 vision of a comfortable dystopia still feels uncomfortably close.'),
  (25, 'L. Ron Hubbard''s 1937 Western, set in 1806. Yellow Hair, a white man raised by the Blackfeet, is caught between his adopted people and the fur traders pushing west. Unusual for its time in telling the story from the Native side.'),
  (26, 'Des Dearlove distils the business lessons behind Richard Branson and Virgin, from building a brand and taking risks to keeping a company entrepreneurial as it grows.'),
  (27, 'Karl Marx''s analysis of how capitalism works: commodities, labour, value, profit and the accumulation of capital. This edition gathers the first two volumes.'),
  (28, 'Painter James Gurney, creator of Dinotopia, explains how light and colour behave in the real world and how to paint them convincingly, using his own paintings as examples.'),
  (29, 'K.M. Weiland shows how a character''s inner change can drive a story''s structure, covering positive, flat and negative arcs with examples from well-known books and films.'),
  (30, 'A Witcher prequel. A young Geralt, barely out of Kaer Morhen, takes his first steps as a monster hunter and learns from an older witcher how the world really works. Sapkowski''s return to the saga after more than a decade.'),
  (31, 'As a child, Libby Day survived the night her family was killed, and her testimony put her brother in prison. Twenty-five years later, broke and adrift, she agrees to revisit the case for a club of true-crime obsessives. Gillian Flynn''s second novel.'),
  (32, 'Altaf Fatima''s classic Urdu novel follows Gaythi, a spirited, unconventional girl growing up in a large family in the decade before Partition. A landmark of Urdu literature, translated into English as The One Who Did Not Ask.'),
  (33, 'Ursa Mackenzie lives in New York but remains bound to the small Caribbean island where her father is a powerful politician. Paule Marshall''s novel explores family, politics and independence across two worlds.'),
  (34, 'Dickens''s most autobiographical novel follows David from a harsh childhood through work, friendship and love to life as a writer, with unforgettable characters such as Mr Micawber and Uriah Heep.'),
  (35, 'Book two of the Malazan Book of the Fallen moves to the Seven Cities, where a prophesied uprising against the empire erupts. Several storylines converge, including a desperate march across the continent that readers still talk about.'),
  (36, 'Chemist Kathryn Harkup investigates the science behind the many deaths in Shakespeare''s plays, from poison and swordplay to the infamous bear, and what Elizabethan audiences would have known about them.'),
  (37, 'The final book of the Three-Body trilogy spans centuries as humanity lives with the consequences of first contact, following an engineer whose choices will shape the future. Vast in scale and packed with ideas.'),
  (38, 'The German edition of Jennifer Donnelly''s The Winter Rose. In 1900 London, newly qualified doctor India Selwyn Jones works among the poor of the East End, where she crosses paths with a notorious gangster. The second Tea Rose novel.'),
  (39, 'Four novellas by Stephen King, mostly away from his usual horror, including the stories behind the films The Shawshank Redemption and Stand by Me.'),
  (40, 'When the NSA''s code-breaking supercomputer meets a code it can''t crack, cryptographer Susan Fletcher is called in to find out why. Dan Brown''s techno-thriller about privacy and surveillance.'),
  (41, 'Steven Pressfield''s short, punchy follow-up to The War of Art: how to beat Resistance and carry a creative project from first idea to finished work.'),
  (42, 'Told entirely in her own voice, Dolores Claiborne''s statement to the police on a small Maine island covers decades of hard work, a bad marriage and the death of her wealthy employer. A gripping character study by Stephen King.'),
  (43, 'Robert Louis Stevenson''s 1886 novella about a respected London doctor and the brutal stranger who seems to have some strange hold over him. A short, chilling classic about the two sides of human nature.'),
  (44, 'Told through letters and diaries, Bram Stoker''s novel follows a young solicitor''s journey to Transylvania and the dark presence that follows him back to England. The novel that defined the vampire.'),
  (45, 'Betty Edwards''s classic course teaches anyone to draw by learning to see differently, with exercises in contours, negative space and proportion. Many readers improve dramatically within weeks.'),
  (46, 'On the desert planet Arrakis, the only source of the most precious substance in the universe, young Paul Atreides and his family are caught in a deadly struggle for power. Frank Herbert''s landmark of science fiction.'),
  (47, 'Mark Manson turns from personal habits to the bigger picture: why hope is fragile, how we make sense of a chaotic world, and what to do when everything feels broken. Irreverent and surprisingly philosophical.'),
  (48, 'Based on Paul Cooper''s popular podcast, this book tells the stories of societies that rose and fell, from the Bronze Age to the Maya and beyond, and asks what their endings have in common.'),
  (49, 'A reclusive author is murdered for his unpublished notebooks, which then lie hidden for decades until a teenage boy digs them up. Retired detective Bill Hodges returns in the second book of Stephen King''s trilogy.'),
  (50, 'Fifteen years after the Farseer trilogy, FitzChivalry lives quietly as a recluse until an old mentor asks him to find a missing prince. The first Tawny Man book by Robin Hobb.'),
  (51, 'Marcos Mateu-Mestre''s guide to technical perspective for illustrators and storyboard artists: how to construct convincing spaces, figures and objects, and use them to tell a story.'),
  (52, 'Nicholas Christopher''s novel follows Franklin Flyer, a gifted young inventor, from the Depression through the Second World War, through success, love and the upheavals of the war years.'),
  (53, 'An issue of John Freeman''s literary anthology devoted to change, gathering stories, essays and poems by writers from around the world.'),
  (54, 'Alvin Toffler''s 1970 bestseller argued that change was arriving faster than people and societies could absorb it. Many of its ideas about technology and information overload proved remarkably prescient.'),
  (55, 'The Malazan Empire turns its sights on Darujhistan, the last free city of its continent, as soldiers, mages, assassins and gods pursue their own plans. The demanding first book of Steven Erikson''s epic fantasy.'),
  (56, 'Nobel-winning economist Joseph Stiglitz, once chief economist at the World Bank, critiques how the IMF and other global institutions treated developing economies in the 1990s, and argues for a fairer globalisation.'),
  (57, 'Pip, an orphan raised by his sister in the Kent marshes, comes into a fortune from an unknown benefactor and is sent to London to become a gentleman. Dickens''s great novel of ambition, love and identity.'),
  (58, 'Harry''s second year at Hogwarts brings a mysterious warning not to return, students found petrified, and rumours of a hidden chamber deep in the castle. Book two of the series.'),
  (59, 'The final Harry Potter book. Harry, Ron and Hermione leave Hogwarts behind for a dangerous quest that will decide the fate of the wizarding world.'),
  (60, 'The Triwizard Tournament comes to Hogwarts, and Harry is chosen as a champion despite being too young to enter. Book four, where the series takes a darker turn.'),
  (61, 'In Harry''s sixth year, Dumbledore begins private lessons into Voldemort''s past, and Harry finds an old potions textbook scribbled with notes by the mysterious Half-Blood Prince.'),
  (62, 'Harry returns for his fifth year to find the wizarding world refusing to believe his warnings, and a new teacher tightening her grip on Hogwarts. The longest book of the series.'),
  (63, 'On his eleventh birthday, Harry Potter learns he''s a wizard and leaves his miserable relatives for Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry. Where it all began.'),
  (64, 'A dangerous prisoner has escaped from Azkaban, the wizard prison, and he seems to be after Harry. Book three, often named the best of the series.'),
  (65, 'The script of the two-part stage play, set nineteen years after the last book. Harry''s son Albus struggles with the weight of his family''s name and strikes up an unlikely friendship. This edition is in German.'),
  (66, 'Five linked stories follow a generation from 1960 to the end of the century, shaped by the Vietnam War. Stephen King at his most nostalgic, with a touch of the uncanny.'),
  (67, 'A German cookbook of hearty mountain-hut cooking from the Alps (Kulinarisches von der Alm): dumplings, soups, roasts and sweet dishes like those served on the alpine pastures.'),
  (68, 'The Roman emperor Claudius, dismissed by his family as a stammering fool, tells the inside story of the scheming, murderous dynasty around him. Robert Graves''s celebrated historical novel.'),
  (69, 'P.D. Ouspensky''s account of his years studying with the mystic G.I. Gurdjieff, setting out the ideas of the Fourth Way: self-observation, consciousness and inner work.'),
  (70, 'Robert Langdon wakes in a Florence hospital with no memory of the past two days, then finds himself on the run, following clues hidden in Dante''s Inferno to stop a global catastrophe.'),
  (71, 'An orphan survives a cruel childhood to become governess at Thornfield Hall, where she falls for its brooding master, Mr Rochester, and begins to suspect the house is hiding something. Charlotte Brontë''s enduring classic.'),
  (72, 'The German edition of Sidney Sheldon''s The Other Side of Midnight: the intertwined lives of two women, a French actress and an American secretary, connected by the same man, from Paris to Washington to Greece.'),
  (73, 'On a remote island, a billionaire has brought dinosaurs back to life for a theme park. A team of experts invited to inspect it discovers what happens when nature refuses to be controlled.'),
  (74, 'Bert Dodson''s practical drawing course, built around exercises that train you to observe, measure and draw what you actually see.'),
  (75, 'A Brigitte cookbook, in German, of simple and affordable recipes for young people cooking for themselves for the first time.'),
  (76, 'One August morning in 1974, a man walks a tightrope between the Twin Towers. Colum McCann weaves together the lives of New Yorkers below, from a radical Irish monk in the Bronx to grieving mothers on Park Avenue.'),
  (77, 'An older, short study by H.C. Dent of Charles Dickens''s life and personality, from his difficult childhood to his fame as the most popular novelist of his age.'),
  (78, 'Joe Coughlin, the rebellious son of a Boston police captain, turns to crime in the Prohibition era and builds a rum-running empire in Florida. Dennis Lehane''s Edgar-winning gangster novel.'),
  (79, 'Nelson Mandela''s autobiography, from his childhood in the Transkei through the struggle against apartheid and 27 years in prison to becoming South Africa''s first democratically elected president.'),
  (80, 'Shy Fanny Price is brought up by wealthy relatives at Mansfield Park, where the arrival of the glamorous Crawford siblings unsettles everyone. Jane Austen''s most serious novel.'),
  (81, 'The private notes of the Roman emperor Marcus Aurelius, written to himself, on duty, mortality and staying calm in the face of what you can''t control. The classic of Stoic philosophy.'),
  (82, 'Adolf Hitler''s political manifesto, written largely in prison after his failed 1923 coup. It sets out the antisemitic, racist ideology and expansionist aims that led to the Second World War and the Holocaust, and is read today as a historical primary source.'),
  (83, 'The sailor Ishmael signs on to the whaler Pequod, whose captain, Ahab, is consumed by his hunt for the white whale that took his leg. Herman Melville''s sprawling, strange masterpiece.'),
  (84, 'In the far future, cities roll across the land on giant wheels, hunting and devouring smaller towns. When a young apprentice is thrown off London, he must survive the wastelands with a scarred girl bent on revenge. The first of Philip Reeve''s series.'),
  (85, 'ZDF heute journal anchor Marietta Slomka explains how German politics actually works, from elections and parliament to coalitions and the chancellery, with humour and insider anecdotes. In German.'),
  (86, 'Gary Karrass''s practical guide to negotiation for salespeople: preparation, tactics and how to reach deals that hold.'),
  (87, 'A burned-out hacker gets one last chance to work in cyberspace, hired by a mysterious employer for an impossible job. William Gibson''s 1984 debut defined cyberpunk and popularised the word ''cyberspace''.'),
  (88, 'Michael Crichton''s thriller about the frontier of genetic engineering, with gene patents, transgenic animals and biotech companies racing for profit. Based on real developments, with an author''s note on the law.'),
  (89, 'Winston Smith rewrites history for the Party at the Ministry of Truth, under the constant watch of Big Brother, and begins a secret rebellion. George Orwell''s defining novel of totalitarianism.'),
  (90, 'Raised as the nephew of a powerful Mafia don, Astorre Viola must protect the family''s legacy when the don is murdered and both the FBI and rival families close in. Mario Puzo''s final novel.'),
  (91, 'Part memoir, part masterclass: Stephen King recalls how he became a writer, then shares his practical advice on the craft. He finished it after being nearly killed by a van in 1999.'),
  (92, 'Emma and Dexter meet on 15 July 1988, the night of their graduation. David Nicholls revisits them on the same day every year for twenty years, through friendship, love, success and disappointment.'),
  (93, 'Joanna Trollope''s novel about stepfamilies: the stepmothers, stepfathers, exes and children trying to make blended families work, and the jealousies and loyalties that come with them.'),
  (94, 'K.M. Weiland''s guide to planning a novel before you write it, showing how an outline can free your creativity rather than stifle it.'),
  (95, 'In the sprawling city-state of New Crobuzon, a renegade scientist accepts a commission from a bird-man who has lost his wings, and sets something terrible loose. China Miéville''s dense, inventive classic of weird fiction.'),
  (96, 'Ernest Norling''s classic 1939 primer explains perspective drawing in simple steps, with hundreds of clear illustrations.'),
  (97, 'In 2045, people escape a grim reality into the virtual world of the OASIS. When its creator dies, he leaves his fortune to whoever can find an Easter egg hidden inside it, and teenager Wade Watts joins the hunt. Packed with 1980s pop culture.'),
  (98, 'Ludwig Fels''s 1987 German novel about Paul Valla, a casual labourer whose dreams of freedom, adventure and the wild turn into a force that wrecks his life. In German.'),
  (99, 'Classicist Mary Beard tells the story of ancient Rome''s first thousand years, from its myths and beginnings to the early empire, questioning much of what we think we know about the Romans.'),
  (100, 'Jessica Brody adapts Blake Snyder''s famous screenwriting method for novelists, with a 15-beat story structure and examples from popular books.'),
  (101, 'Coursebook and workbook in one for German learners working through level B1 (first half), from Hueber''s Schritte plus Neu series.'),
  (102, 'Coursebook and workbook in one for German learners completing level B1, from Hueber''s Schritte plus Neu series.'),
  (103, 'The final volume of Tad Williams''s Otherland, where the struggle inside a vast virtual-reality network, and against the powerful people who built it, reaches its conclusion.'),
  (104, 'A standalone Witcher novel set between the early short stories. Geralt loses his swords and gets tangled up in the intrigues of a coastal kingdom and a sorceress.'),
  (105, 'Sisters Elinor and Marianne Dashwood, one sensible and one passionate, face reduced circumstances and heartbreak after their father''s death. Jane Austen''s first published novel.'),
  (106, 'Bill Bryson''s short, entertaining biography of Shakespeare, sorting the little we actually know from the centuries of guesswork.'),
  (107, 'In 1980s Glasgow, young Shuggie Bain grows up devoted to his mother, Agnes, as she battles alcoholism in a city hit hard by poverty. Douglas Stuart''s Booker-winning debut.'),
  (108, 'In 1600, English navigator John Blackthorne is shipwrecked in Japan and drawn into the power struggle between feudal lords and the rivalry of European traders. James Clavell''s epic of clashing cultures.'),
  (109, 'When a lobbyist tosses fast-food rubbish out of his car, an eco-avenger named Twilly Spree decides to teach him a lesson. Carl Hiaasen''s comic Florida caper, with the wild ex-governor Skink.'),
  (110, 'Three thousand years after Ender''s Game, Ender travels to a colony world where humans have met a second alien species, and a misunderstanding threatens disaster. The second book of Orson Scott Card''s saga.'),
  (111, 'Google Ventures'' five-day process for answering big business questions: map the problem, sketch solutions, decide, build a prototype and test it with real customers.'),
  (112, 'A step-by-step guide to setting up an import/export company, from finding products and partners to paperwork, shipping and getting paid.'),
  (113, 'K.M. Weiland explains classic story structure, from the hook and the key plot points down to building individual scenes, for writers who want their novels to work.'),
  (114, 'The second collection of Witcher short stories, in which Geralt joins a dragon hunt, crosses paths with Yennefer again, and meets a girl whose destiny is tied to his.'),
  (115, 'Tom Sawyer grows up on the Mississippi in the 1840s, skipping school, whitewashing fences and getting caught up in a real crime with Huckleberry Finn. Mark Twain''s classic of boyhood.'),
  (116, 'Santiago, an Andalusian shepherd boy, dreams of treasure at the Egyptian pyramids and sets off to find it. Paulo Coelho''s fable about following your dreams.'),
  (117, 'A prequel to The Hunger Games, set at the tenth Games. Eighteen-year-old Coriolanus Snow is assigned to mentor a girl from District 12, and his own future depends on how she does.'),
  (118, 'Robert H. Schuller reads the eight Beatitudes from the Sermon on the Mount as positive attitudes for a happier life.'),
  (119, 'A collection of H.P. Lovecraft''s cosmic horror, including The Call of Cthulhu, The Dunwich Horror and At the Mountains of Madness.'),
  (120, 'Mae Holland lands a job at the Circle, the world''s most powerful tech company, and is swept up in its campaign for total transparency. Dave Eggers''s satire of social media and surveillance.'),
  (121, 'Dallas lawyer Scott Fenney has it all until a federal judge appoints him to defend a Black prostitute accused of murdering a senator''s son. A legal thriller often compared to To Kill a Mockingbird.'),
  (122, 'Marx and Engels''s 1848 pamphlet: a short, forceful account of history as class struggle, ending with a call for the workers of the world to unite. One of the most influential political texts ever written.'),
  (123, 'Arrested at his own wedding feast, Edmond Dantès is imprisoned for a crime he didn''t commit. Years later he re-emerges, rich and transformed, with a plan. Alexandre Dumas''s great adventure of revenge.'),
  (124, 'When a supermodel falls to her death, the police call it suicide, but her brother hires private detective Cormoran Strike, a war veteran down on his luck. The first Strike mystery, by J.K. Rowling writing as Robert Galbraith.'),
  (125, 'Harvard symbologist Robert Langdon is called to the Louvre after a murder and finds a trail of codes hidden in the works of Leonardo da Vinci. The bestselling thriller of art, religion and secret societies.'),
  (126, 'Book two of the Three-Body trilogy. Humanity has four centuries before an alien fleet arrives, and an unlikely academic is chosen for a secret strategy to save it.'),
  (127, 'Most of the world is blinded overnight after a strange green meteor shower, and the triffids, walking venomous plants, begin to roam free. John Wyndham''s classic of British science fiction.'),
  (128, 'Isaac Asimov''s earliest short stories, from 1939 onwards, with his own lively commentary on writing them and selling them to the pulp magazines.'),
  (129, 'Nino Haratischwili''s epic follows a Georgian family through the 20th century, from a secret chocolate recipe to revolution, war and the Soviet empire. Told by a woman to her young niece, Brilka.'),
  (130, 'Robert Bringhurst''s authoritative and beautifully written guide to typography: choosing typefaces, spacing, page layout and the history behind them.'),
  (131, 'Tiffy needs somewhere cheap to live; Leon works nights and needs the money. Sharing a one-bed flat on opposite schedules, they only communicate through notes. Beth O''Leary''s romantic comedy.'),
  (132, 'Richard Rudd''s guide to contemplation based on 64 ''Gene Keys'', each describing a pattern that can move from shadow to gift to its highest potential, drawing on the I Ching.'),
  (133, 'Driven off their Oklahoma farm during the Dust Bowl, the Joad family heads for California in search of work and dignity. John Steinbeck''s Pulitzer-winning novel of the Great Depression.'),
  (134, 'The first three Dune novels in one volume: Dune, Dune Messiah and Children of Dune.'),
  (135, 'Venture capitalist Ben Horowitz shares hard-won lessons from building and running companies, with candid advice on the problems business schools don''t cover.'),
  (136, 'In 1960s Jackson, Mississippi, a young white woman and two Black maids secretly work together on a book about what it''s like to work for white families. Kathryn Stockett''s bestseller.'),
  (137, 'Plantagenet Somerset Fry''s illustrated history of the world, from the earliest civilisations to the modern age, for curious readers of all ages.'),
  (138, 'All five novels of Douglas Adams''s comic science-fiction saga in one hardcover, starting minutes before Earth is demolished to make way for a hyperspace bypass.'),
  (139, 'Bilbo Baggins, a comfort-loving hobbit, is swept off by the wizard Gandalf and thirteen dwarves on a quest to win back their treasure from the dragon Smaug. Tolkien''s classic adventure.'),
  (140, 'In Panem, children are chosen every year to fight to the death on live television. When Katniss Everdeen volunteers to take her little sister''s place, she has to find a way to survive. The first book of the trilogy.'),
  (141, 'Proceedings of the History for Peace conferences on teaching history (Calcutta 2019 and Pune 2020), with contributors including Romila Thapar, Gautam Bhatia and A.G. Noorani on the making and values of India''s constitution.'),
  (142, 'Amir grows up in Kabul alongside Hassan, his father''s servant''s son, until a betrayal in 1975 haunts him for decades. Khaled Hosseini''s story of friendship, guilt and redemption across Afghanistan''s turbulent history.'),
  (143, 'The fifth and final novel of the Witcher saga, in which the stories of Geralt, Yennefer and Ciri reach their conclusion.'),
  (144, 'Mario Puzo follows the Clericuzio crime family as it tries to go legitimate through Las Vegas casinos and Hollywood studios. A sprawling saga of loyalty and power.'),
  (145, 'The first collection of Witcher stories introduces Geralt of Rivia, a monster hunter for hire, in dark, twisted retellings of fairy tales. The best place to start the series.'),
  (146, 'Holistic detective Dirk Gently investigates an exploding check-in desk at Heathrow and soon finds himself dealing with Norse gods adrift in modern England. Douglas Adams at his most absurd.'),
  (147, 'Frodo Baggins inherits a ring of terrible power and must carry it across Middle-earth to destroy it before the Dark Lord Sauron can reclaim it. Tolkien''s complete epic in one volume.'),
  (148, 'To convince her friend she''s moved on, PhD student Olive Smith kisses the first man she sees, who turns out to be the notoriously grumpy professor Adam Carlsen. A fake-dating romance set in academia.'),
  (149, 'When one of his patients is murdered, New York psychoanalyst Judd Stevens realises he may have been the real target. Sidney Sheldon''s first novel, a tight psychological thriller.'),
  (150, 'Surgeon Dorrigo Evans, a prisoner of war forced to build the Burma Death Railway, is haunted by that ordeal and by a love affair from before the war. Richard Flanagan''s Booker-winning novel.'),
  (151, 'A big selection of Charles Bukowski''s poems from 1951 to 1993: raw, funny and bleak verse about drink, women, work and writing.'),
  (152, 'A guide by Babson College professors to the essentials of starting and growing a business: spotting opportunities, planning, financing, marketing and more.'),
  (153, 'Isabel Archer, a spirited young American, inherits a fortune and travels to Europe determined to shape her own destiny, only to find freedom harder to keep than to win. Henry James''s masterpiece.'),
  (154, 'Essays and interviews from psychologists, philosophers and writers on why clever people do foolish things, and how stupidity works in individuals and societies. Edited by Jean-François Marmion.'),
  (155, 'In a Lahore café, a young Pakistani man tells an American stranger about his years at Princeton and a high-flying New York job, and how everything changed after 9/11. Mohsin Hamid''s tense, Booker-shortlisted monologue.'),
  (156, 'Plato''s dialogue in which Socrates asks what justice is and imagines an ideal city to find out. Home of the allegory of the cave and the philosopher king.'),
  (157, 'Simple rules for building wealth, told as parables set in ancient Babylon: save part of what you earn, make it work for you, and avoid losing it. A 1926 classic of personal finance.'),
  (158, 'George Orwell''s 1937 report on life among the working poor in the mining towns of northern England, followed by his argument for socialism.'),
  (159, 'Genetics professor Don Tillman sets out to find a wife scientifically, with a sixteen-page questionnaire. Then Rosie, who fails it on every count, arrives with a project of her own. A warm romantic comedy.'),
  (160, 'Naomi Klein argues that governments and corporations have used crises such as wars, coups and natural disasters to push through unpopular free-market policies while people are too disoriented to resist.'),
  (161, 'FBI trainee Clarice Starling seeks the help of the brilliant, imprisoned psychiatrist Hannibal Lecter to catch a serial killer. Thomas Harris''s chilling thriller.'),
  (162, 'Peter Frankopan retells world history from the East, following the trade routes that linked Europe and Asia and showing how they shaped empires, religions and economies.'),
  (163, 'After a weaponised flu wipes out most of humanity, the survivors are drawn into a struggle between good and evil across a ruined America. Stephen King''s epic of the end of the world.'),
  (164, 'In 1980s East Germany, the Stasi ran a poetry circle for its own officers. Philip Oltermann tells this strange true story to show how the regime tried to turn literature into a weapon.'),
  (165, 'Historian Michael Wood''s companion to his BBC series tells the history of China through its people, places and ideas, from the ancient dynasties to the present.'),
  (166, 'Annie Leonard follows the life cycle of everyday things, from extraction and production to consumption and waste, and what it all costs people and the planet. This edition is in German.'),
  (167, 'Steven Silbiger condenses the core subjects taught at top business schools, from marketing and finance to accounting and strategy, into ten digestible days.'),
  (168, 'It''s the final Test of the Ashes, and stand-in England captain James McCall has to hold an exhausted team together. Written by England''s cricket analyst, a rare novel that captures life inside a professional dressing room.'),
  (169, 'During China''s Cultural Revolution, a secret military project sends signals into space. Decades later, a scientist is drawn into a strange virtual-reality game and a mystery surrounding a string of physicists. The first book of Cixin Liu''s acclaimed trilogy.'),
  (170, 'Four pensioners in a retirement village meet every Thursday to pore over unsolved cases, until a real murder happens on their doorstep. Richard Osman''s cosy and very funny mystery.'),
  (171, 'Malcolm Gladwell explains how ideas, products and behaviours spread like epidemics, and the small things that make them tip into the mainstream.'),
  (172, 'Book four of the Witcher saga. Wounded and alone, Ciri is taken in by a hermit and tells him her story, while Geralt and his companions keep searching for her.'),
  (173, 'All five Hitchhiker''s novels plus the short story Young Zaphod Plays It Safe in one volume. Don''t panic.'),
  (174, 'In Prague around the 1968 Soviet invasion, a surgeon, his wife and his lover live through love, infidelity and politics. Milan Kundera''s philosophical novel about lightness, weight and choice.'),
  (175, 'Clay Jensen receives a box of cassette tapes recorded by Hannah Baker, a classmate who took her own life, explaining her thirteen reasons. A young adult novel about difficult themes, including suicide.'),
  (176, 'Book two of the Witcher saga. Ciri travels with Yennefer towards the mages'' school, but a gathering of sorcerers on the island of Thanedd turns into a coup, and the Continent slides into war.'),
  (177, 'Henry Fielding''s comic 1749 novel follows Tom, a good-hearted foundling with a weakness for women, through scrapes and adventures across England in pursuit of his beloved Sophia.'),
  (178, 'Two misfit fourteen-year-olds from Berlin, Maik and the Russian-born Tschick, set off on a summer road trip in a borrowed (well, stolen) Lada with no map and no plan. A funny, touching German classic about growing up. In German.'),
  (179, 'When Bella Swan moves to rainy Forks, Washington, she becomes fascinated by the mysterious Edward Cullen and his strange family. The first book of the Twilight saga.'),
  (180, 'Harriet Beecher Stowe''s 1852 anti-slavery novel follows Tom, an enslaved man sold away from his family, and Eliza''s desperate escape with her child. It had an enormous influence on American opinion before the Civil War.'),
  (181, 'A Klett business German coursebook for intermediate learners, with workplace scenarios, vocabulary and communication practice.'),
  (182, 'Franz Hessel''s 1929 classic of the flâneur: leisurely walks through the streets, cafés and courtyards of Weimar-era Berlin, sharply observed. In English translation.'),
  (183, 'Tolstoy''s epic of Russian society during the Napoleonic Wars follows Pierre, Andrei and Natasha through love, battle and the search for meaning.'),
  (184, 'Philosopher William MacAskill makes the case for longtermism: the idea that positively influencing the long-term future is a key moral priority of our time.'),
  (185, 'Short biographies of 75 remarkable Irish women from history, from pirates and queens to scientists, writers and rebels.'),
  (186, 'Jakob Friedrich Fries''s 1805 philosophical work on three ways of holding something true: knowledge, faith and ''Ahndung'', a felt intimation of the eternal in nature and beauty. In German.'),
  (187, 'The sequel to Speaker for the Dead. A deadly virus threatens the colony world of Lusitania, while a fleet approaches with orders to destroy the planet. Ender faces his hardest moral choices yet.'),
  (188, 'A 2024 collection of twelve Stephen King stories and novellas, including a sequel to Cujo and the tale of a man who dreams where a body is buried.')
) as v (id, description)
where b.id = v.id;

